  "title": "Dijo --- The Stem That Breaks Its Own Rule",
  "drivablePrefix": 1,
  "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:9e24eba18f57a2ff",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "ES-C57-dijo",
      "sequence": 2295,
      "title": "dijo",
      "headword": "dijo",
      "romanization": "dijo",
      "gloss": "he said — a new strong stem, which obeys the pattern in the singular and then breaks it in the plural",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:15ee2519458b6462",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The strong pattern, once more: a stem that changed shape, and then endings that carry no stress. (Tuve, tuvo.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more stem is about to join them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the stem is dij-",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Decir builds its past on dij-, and in the singular nothing is new at all:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                ""
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 2,
              "utterances": [
                "tuve, tuvo, the stem you know.",
                "dije, dijo, the same endings, on a new stem."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Unstressed at the end, exactly like the others. Dijo, not dijó."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin said dīxit. That x stood for a [ks] — the sound at the end of English box."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Old Spanish kept the letter and softened the sound: dixo, with the x said roughly like English sh. Centuries later that sound hardened at the back of the mouth into the one j spells today, and the spelling followed the sound. dīxit becomes dixo becomes dijo."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So the j in dijo is not a decoration. It is a Latin x, wearing modern clothes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: where it breaks the pattern",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You learned the strong plural as stem plus -ieron: tuvieron, hicieron, estuvieron. Apply that here and you would get dijieron."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is dijeron. The i is gone."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The i in -ieron is not a full vowel — it is a glide, the y sound of English yes, leaning on the vowel after it. And Spanish does not keep a glide next to a sound already made in that part of the mouth. The j was there first, so the glide goes."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This is not a fact about decir. Any strong stem ending in j does it, and you will meet more of them; when you do, the ending will already be familiar."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
